    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel frustrated and look for explanations after getting so close to a senior appointment, especially with your strong background and decade of civil service experience. \n\nHowever, **you should not make an issue of the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women, nor does it inherently signify bias.**\n\nHere is an analysis of why, along with what you should consider doing next:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does an all-women panel indicate bias?\n* **Role-based appointments:** In civil service recruitment, selection panels are typically composed based on specific organizational roles—often the hiring director, an HR representative, a peer from a related policy unit, and sometimes an independent assessor. If those roles happen to be occupied by women, the panel will naturally be all-female. \n* **The mirror standard:** In the past (and still today), many interview panels were composed entirely of men without it automatically indicating a conspiracy or bias against female candidates. The same principle applies in reverse: composition alone is not evidence of discrimination.\n* **Internal policies on panel diversity:** Some public services have guidelines encouraging mixed-gender panels where feasible. However, an all-female panel rarely breaches formal recruitment rules unless your jurisdiction has an explicit, mandatory quota rule that was breached.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Paper qualifications vs. final-stage selection\nAt the final interview stage of a shortlist of three:\n* **All candidates are qualified on paper:** You have strong credentials (law degree, MPP, 10 years of experience), but the successful candidate also has relevant qualifications (MA in political science) and targeted experience (\"leading international projects\"). \n* **Strategic priorities matter:** Ministries often look for specific capabilities tailored to upcoming initiatives. If the ministry is expanding its bilateral, multilateral, or cross-border work, international project leadership may have been the decisive factor.\n* **Interview performance:** Final decisions in the civil service almost always come down to the scores against competency frameworks during the interview itself (e.g., strategic vision, problem-solving under questioning, communication style) rather than years in post.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The risks of raising the issue\n* **High reputational cost:** Suggesting that you were not selected because the panel was all-female—without concrete evidence of discriminatory conduct—is likely to be viewed as unconstructive, defensive, or an unsubstantiated accusation. In a civil service environment, this can unnecessarily damage professional relationships and future prospects.\n* **Lack of actionable ground:** Legally and procedurally, the burden of proof for unlawful discrimination requires demonstrable bias (e.g., improper scoring, inappropriate interview questions, or a clear deviation from standard evaluation rubrics). An all-female panel alone does not meet this threshold.\n\n---\n\n### 4. The two-week delay in written feedback\n* A two-week wait for written feedback in a government ministry is standard and does not indicate foul play. \n* Panel members have heavy operational workloads, and formal written debriefs often require sign-off from HR or the panel chair before release.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended next steps\n\n1. **Follow up politely on the feedback:**\n   Send a brief, constructive follow-up to the HR coordinator or panel chair:\n   > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Senior Policy Adviser role. I understand how busy the department is, but I would greatly appreciate any written feedback or scoring notes from the panel when available, as I am keen to identify areas to strengthen for future senior opportunities.\"*\n\n2. **Request an oral debrief if possible:**\n   Written summaries are often sanitized and formulaic. If offered, an informal 15-minute phone call with the panel chair often yields much more candid, actionable insight into where the successful candidate edged you out.\n\n3. **Examine the scoring, not the panel’s demographics:**\n   Once you receive the competency scores and notes, review whether there were specific questions or competencies where you scored lower than expected. \n\n4. **Keep your union or staff association in reserve:**\n   If the formal feedback reveals genuine procedural irregularities (such as criteria being applied that were not in the job specification, or standard scoring guidelines being ignored), that would be the time to consult a civil service union representative—focusing strictly on process and criteria, not gender.\n",
